     "content": "### Offer Evaluation\n\nOn paper, this is a **solid standard first offer for an L6 PM at Google**, but it leaves significant room on the table\u2014especially given that Google reached out to you and you are happy at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### The Financial Breakdown\n\nGoogle utilizes a front-loaded **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** four-year vesting schedule for new-hire equity (GSUs):\n\n| Metric | Meta IC6 (Current) | Google L6 (Offer) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (~15%) | $47,000 (20%) |\n| **Year 1 Equity** | ~$200,000\u2013$250,000 | **$356,400** (33% of $1.08M) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 2 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 3 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 | **~$520,000** + Refreshes |\n| **Year 4 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 | **~$411,600** + Refreshes |\n| **4-Year Annual Average (Flat)** | **~$526,000/yr** | **~$552,000/yr** |\n\n#### Key Insights from the Numbers\n1. **Front-Loaded Upside:** In Years 1 and 2, your take-home total compensation at Google would be ~**$112,000/year higher** than your baseline at Meta.\n2. **The Year 3\u20134 Cliff:** Because of Google's front-loading, your base grant equity drops in Years 3 and 4. While annual refreshers stack to offset this, recruiter refreshes are performance-dependent and never contractually guaranteed.\n3. **Unnecessary Concessions:** You are taking a **$5,000 cut on base salary**, and Google has included **$0 in sign-on bonus** to compensate for leaving your existing unvested pipeline.\n\n---\n\n### Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet. You should negotiate.**\n\nYou hold maximum leverage in this situation:\n* You were sourced passively.\n* You are already well-compensated and happy at your current company.\n* Google L6 PM compensation bands reach well above this initial calibration (top of band often pushes $600k\u2013$650k+ annualized).\n\n---\n\n### What Exactly to Ask For\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter-Offer | Why |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Bridges the transition, covers your upcoming Meta bonus/vest cycle, and requires no permanent budget band approval from compensation committees. |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000 \u2013 $255,000** | Your current base is $240,000. Google's posted range goes up to $271,000, so $250k+ is well within the band. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr GSU)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Increases your average annual equity from $270k/yr to ~$315k\u2013$325k/yr, protecting against the Year 3\u20134 drop. |\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Script for the Recruiter\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed the team conversations, and the scope of the L6 role is exciting. That said, as we discussed early on, I\u2019m in a great position at Meta where I'm tracking at roughly $526k total comp with $240k base.*\n>\n> *Looking at this package, the base salary is slightly below what I currently make, and with no sign-on bonus, leaving my upcoming vests and performance cycle creates friction.*\n>\n> *If we can bring the **base salary to $250,000**, add a **$80,000 sign-on bonus**, and adjust the equity grant to **$1.25M**, I would feel completely confident making the jump and would sign immediately.\"*",
