     "content": "### The Revised Offer Breakdown\n\nGoogle raised the equity grant by **$200,000** (from $1.08M to $1.28M) and matched your Meta base salary at **$240,000**. They did not provide a separate cash sign-on bonus because they chose to address your unvested $200k by rolling that exact value directly into your RSU pool.\n\nUnder Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule (**33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**):\n\n| Year | Base Salary | Target Bonus (20%) | Initial Equity Vest | Google Total Comp | Meta Baseline | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 (33%) | **$710,400** | ~$526,000 | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 (33%) | **$710,400** | ~$526,000 | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 3** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $281,600 (22%) | **$569,600** + refreshes | ~$526,000 | **+$43,600+** |\n| **Year 4** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $153,600 (12%) | **$441,600** + refreshes | ~$526,000 | Refreshes decide |\n| **4-Yr Avg**| **$240,000** | **$48,000** | **$320,000/yr** | **$608,000/yr** | **$526,000/yr** | **+$82,000/yr** |\n\n---\n\n### Market Calibration & Compensation Assessment\n\n* **Band Reality:** An annualized average of **$608,000/yr** (and over **$710k in Years 1 & 2**) places this offer in the **top 10\u201315% (75th\u201390th percentile)** of Google L6 PM packages in the Bay Area.\n* **The \"Lost\" $200k RSUs:** Google's Year 1 stock payout alone is **$422,400**, which fully absorbs the $200k you would forfeit at Meta while leaving you with an extra **$222,400 in Year 1 stock** on top.\n* **Cash Flow Advantage:** In your first two years, you will take home roughly **$368,000 more** in total compensation than you would have staying at your current Meta run-rate.\n\n---\n\n### Should You Take It?\n\nFrom a purely financial standpoint, **this is an exceptionally strong offer that has reached the ceiling of what Google typically grants without an active, higher competing offer.** \n\nBecause you mentioned that you are happy at Meta and weren't originally looking, your final decision should hinge on non-monetary factors:\n\n#### Reasons to Accept\n1. **Financial Realization:** You secure an additional ~$370k in gross comp across Years 1 & 2 alone.\n2. **Career Capital / Resume Diversification:** Having Senior/Staff-level PM ownership on your resume at both Meta and Google is rare and provides tremendous long-term market durability.\n3. **Culture / Pace:** If you want a change of pace from Meta\u2019s high-intensity performance cycles (PSC), Google generally offers a more sustainable work-life balance and broader scope across platform/enterprise products.\n\n#### Reasons to Stay at Meta\n1. **You are Tracking for Promotion (IC7):** If you are within 12\u201318 months of reaching IC7 (Principal PM) at Meta, jumping to Google as an L6 resets your internal promotion clock. Meta IC7 total compensation typically ranges between $800k\u2013$1M+.\n2. **Impact & Velocity:** If you prefer Meta's fast ship velocity and bottom-up culture over Google's consensus-driven approval structure.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer.** \n\nThe compensation committee delivered a top-tier package for L6. Unless you have an imminent IC7 promotion locked in at Meta within the next performance cycle, the upside of taking home **>$700k/year for the next two years** makes this move well worth making.",
